Vanilla could use a _lot_ of tweaks.

My primary objection to blithely churning out new random content is that
it doesn't add any challenge to the vanilla game as it currently stands, 
just more places to go and snowplow underneath your character's gigantic,
hobnailed boots.

New content isn't bad at all.  It just has to matter first.

The reason it's hard to add challenge to the game is because certain
binary mechanics are inherently overpowered, rendering huge portions of
the game useless.  Martin mentioned dragons, but the change didn't
specifically target dragons, it targeted reflection -- dragons are just
one of the many beneficiaries of this.

There are two ways to add complexity.  One is to bolt on more and more
content until you end up with Slash'EM; this is a direction I refuse to
go.  Another is to enhance what content you have so that it actually
matters.  The "destroy armor" changes do this to a great degree -- if
nothing else, they make spending enchant armor scrolls on fooproofing 
or repairing your armor actually worthwhile, unlike vanilla.

(Food for thought: Did you know that a thoroughly rotted thoroughly
burnt +7 elven leather helm provides 7 points of AC?  

Did you know that no monster in vanilla can destroy that helm while it's
on your head, other than using the spell of destroy armor -- which magic
resistance protects against 100%?

Did you know that the same helm, fireproof, will only provide 8 points
of AC -- meaning that your scroll of enchant armor would have been
better spent enchanting some other piece of armor that wasn't maxed out
yet?)

